WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.421
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4426.

2
00:00:01.421 --> 00:00:02.842
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:02.842 --> 00:00:03.316
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>Alright.

4
00:00:03.316 --> 00:00:05.684
Today is March 27, 2015.

5
00:00:05.684 --> 00:00:13.263
And I’m Chelsea Arseneault representing the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU Libraries.

6
00:00:13.263 --> 00:00:20.842
We are working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum to document the history of Mossville.

7
00:00:20.842 --> 00:00:26.053
Today we are at the Rigmaiden Center on Old Spanish Trail.

8
00:00:26.053 --> 00:00:31.263
I’m here today with Ms. Carolyn Marshall and Ms. Shirley Andrus.

9
00:00:31.263 --> 00:00:36.000
Thank you all so much for agreeing to be interviewed.

10
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:42.158
This is going to be a short interview, about 15 to 20 minutes.

11
00:00:42.158 --> 00:00:51.158
And we will likely be in touch with you later to set up a longer interview, if you’re interested.

12
00:00:51.158 --> 00:00:51.632
<v CAROLYN MARSHALL>Okay.

13
00:00:51.632 --> 00:00:52.579
That’s fine.

14
00:00:52.579 --> 00:00:55.895
<v ARSENEAULT>Could you please state your full names?

15
00:00:55.895 --> 00:00:57.316
<v SHIRLEY ANDRUS>Shirley Simien Andrus.

16
00:00:57.316 --> 00:00:58.737
<v MARSHALL>Carolyn Simien Marshall.

17
00:00:58.737 --> 00:01:01.579
<v ARSENEAULT>When and where were you born?

18
00:01:01.579 --> 00:01:05.368
<v ANDRUS>We were born right here in Mossville, Louisiana.

19
00:01:05.368 --> 00:01:09.158
<v MARSHALL>I was born May 21, 1956, in Mossville.

20
00:01:09.158 --> 00:01:12.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What are your parents’ names?

21
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:17.306
<v ANDRUS>Our father and mother was Lawrence James Simien, Senior, and Beatrice Hardy Simien.

22
00:01:17.306 --> 00:01:18.531
<v ARSENEAULT>And your grandparents?

23
00:01:18.531 --> 00:01:22.204
<v ANDRUS>Our grandparents was [Zem?] Simien and Teresa Fontenot Simien

24
00:01:22.204 --> 00:01:22.612
<v ARSENEAULT>On?

25
00:01:22.612 --> 00:01:23.429
<v MARSHALL>Daddy’s side.

26
00:01:23.429 --> 00:01:24.653
<v ANDRUS>My dad’s side.

27
00:01:24.653 --> 00:01:28.327
On my mom’s side was [Arzina Hardy?], Senior, and—

28
00:01:28.327 --> 00:01:28.735
<v MARSHALL>Mary.

29
00:01:28.735 --> 00:01:29.959
<v ANDRUS>Mary Collins Simien.

30
00:01:29.959 --> 00:01:30.776
I mean—

31
00:01:30.776 --> 00:01:32.000
<v MARSHALL>Hardy. (laughs)

32
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:32.317
<v ANDRUS>Hardy.

33
00:01:32.317 --> 00:01:34.537
I’m getting them all mixed up now.

34
00:01:34.537 --> 00:01:37.073
<v ARSENEAULT>So what is your earliest memory of Mossville?

35
00:01:37.073 --> 00:01:38.024
<v MARSHALL>Oh my God!

36
00:01:38.024 --> 00:01:43.732
<v ANDRUS>My earliest memory of Mossville as I was coming up as a child is Mossville community was family-oriented.

37
00:01:43.732 --> 00:01:45.634
Family that got together to celebrate.

38
00:01:45.634 --> 00:01:48.805
Family that got together if you needed help or whatever.

39
00:01:48.805 --> 00:01:52.610
Then my father, his dream was always to open a grocery store.

40
00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:57.366
He opened a little community grocery store to help the people out in the neighborhood.

41
00:01:57.366 --> 00:02:05.293
He didn’t care if you ain’t had the money or whatever, he let you charge and stuff, and then you come pay when you can.

42
00:02:05.293 --> 00:02:11.000
If you had to put something on it, he still wouldn’t turn his back on you or nothing.

43
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:12.902
He said, come on and charge.

44
00:02:12.902 --> 00:02:16.707
I can remember the time when we was all kids coming up,

45
00:02:16.707 --> 00:02:21.463
in the store, the price of food was much cheaper then than it is now.

46
00:02:21.463 --> 00:02:22.415
<v MARSHALL>Oh, lord, yes!

47
00:02:22.415 --> 00:02:22.732
<v ANDRUS>Okay?

48
00:02:22.732 --> 00:02:25.902
Because during that Depression time, when we was coming up. . .

49
00:02:25.902 --> 00:02:28.122
<v MARSHALL>A penny for a bubble gum. [laughs]

50
00:02:28.122 --> 00:02:30.024
<v ANDRUS>A penny for a bubble gum.

51
00:02:30.024 --> 00:02:32.244
A penny for any kind of candy.

52
00:02:32.244 --> 00:02:34.463
A nickel for a can of soda.

53
00:02:34.463 --> 00:02:37.000
Seventy-five cents for a pound of lunchmeat.

54
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:37.725
<v MARSHALL>Oh, yes!

55
00:02:37.725 --> 00:02:39.536
A pound of lunchmeat. [laughs]

56
00:02:39.536 --> 00:02:40.623
<v ANDRUS>It was awesome.

57
00:02:40.623 --> 00:02:42.435
Milk wasn’t but 89 cents.

58
00:02:42.435 --> 00:02:43.159
<v MARSHALL>Eighty-nine cents.

59
00:02:43.159 --> 00:02:43.884
<v ANDRUS>A gallon.

60
00:02:43.884 --> 00:02:45.333
<v MARSHALL>It was good then.

61
00:02:45.333 --> 00:02:46.420
<v ANDRUS>It was good.

62
00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:50.406
It was really, we really enjoyed living in here in Mossville.

63
00:02:50.406 --> 00:02:52.580
We enjoyed just being family-oriented, period.

64
00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:55.841
We didn’t go to Mossville High School or whatever.

65
00:02:55.841 --> 00:02:59.464
Our parents worked until they put us in Catholic school.

66
00:02:59.464 --> 00:03:04.899
All our family went to Sacred Heart Catholic School, which is in Lake Charles, Louisiana.

67
00:03:04.899 --> 00:03:07.797
Most of Mossville, y’all ain’t going to believe—

68
00:03:07.797 --> 00:03:09.246
<v MARSHALL>Is kin people. [laughs]

69
00:03:09.246 --> 00:03:10.696
<v ANDRUS>Is all kin folks.

70
00:03:10.696 --> 00:03:13.232
Is kin folks on the Simien side.

71
00:03:13.232 --> 00:03:13.957
<v MARSHALL>Everybody kin.

72
00:03:13.957 --> 00:03:14.681
Everybody kin.

73
00:03:14.681 --> 00:03:16.493
<v ANDRUS>It’s kin on the Hardy.

74
00:03:16.493 --> 00:03:19.391
<v MARSHALL>All my mama’s people, all down Spanish Trail.

75
00:03:19.391 --> 00:03:20.478
All the Hardy’s.

76
00:03:20.478 --> 00:03:20.841
<v ANDRUS>Everybody’s.

77
00:03:20.841 --> 00:03:21.203
<v MARSHALL>Everybody.

78
00:03:21.203 --> 00:03:24.101
<v ANDRUS>Spanish Trail, Prater Road, everywhere, is just relatives.

79
00:03:24.101 --> 00:03:25.551
Everything is just [unclear]

80
00:03:25.551 --> 00:03:27.000
<v MARSHALL>And every holiday—

81
00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:27.722
<v ANDRUS>Everybody get together.

82
00:03:27.722 --> 00:03:28.443
<v MARSHALL>One big gathering.

83
00:03:28.443 --> 00:03:29.405
Oh, it was nice.

84
00:03:29.405 --> 00:03:33.013
<v ANDRUS>Everybody bring a couple of dish or whatever, and then we just sit out there.

85
00:03:33.013 --> 00:03:36.139
<v MARSHALL>And everybody, kids would go out there and play kickball against the grown-ups.

86
00:03:36.139 --> 00:03:36.380
<v ANDRUS>Yeah.

87
00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:37.823
<v MARSHALL>Oh, we used to have fun.

88
00:03:37.823 --> 00:03:38.544
<v ANDRUS>We had fun.

89
00:03:38.544 --> 00:03:39.747
<v MARSHALL>Oh, just the good days.

90
00:03:39.747 --> 00:03:41.671
<v ANDRUS>But you don’t find that too much now.

91
00:03:41.671 --> 00:03:41.911
<v MARSHALL>No.

92
00:03:41.911 --> 00:03:43.835
<v ANDRUS>Everybody’s off today in their own little world.

93
00:03:43.835 --> 00:03:46.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What was the name of your dad’s store?

94
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:48.615
<v ANDRUS>My dad’s store was called Simien Grocery Store.

95
00:03:48.615 --> 00:03:50.904
It was located on Old Spanish Trail.

96
00:03:50.904 --> 00:03:53.519
The store was in front of the house.

97
00:03:53.519 --> 00:03:55.481
And we lived in the back.

98
00:03:55.481 --> 00:04:02.019
That’s something that his dream was always to open a store, like I said, just to help the community out.

99
00:04:02.019 --> 00:04:04.308
And he did, until he took sick.

100
00:04:04.308 --> 00:04:11.827
And by us all going to school across the bridge at a Catholic school, some days we wouldn’t make it across the bridge,

101
00:04:11.827 --> 00:04:17.712
especially when we had ice on the bridge or whatever, we had to stay on the other side.

102
00:04:17.712 --> 00:04:27.519
And most of the time it was just Mama at home running the store while Dad and the kids were trying to find a place to sleep across the bridge.

103
00:04:27.519 --> 00:04:29.154
<v MARSHALL>Across the bridge, yeah. [laughter]

104
00:04:29.154 --> 00:04:33.731
<v ANDRUS>Thank God we had other relatives that we was safe to go to [laughter].

105
00:04:33.731 --> 00:04:37.327
But after that, Dad took sick, Dad took it with diabetes.

106
00:04:37.327 --> 00:04:41.250
And he wasn’t able to run the store, take us to school.

107
00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:46.808
And then Mom decided she wanted to go out and find her a job, so she did.

108
00:04:46.808 --> 00:04:51.058
She started working through the Catholic schools just as well, being a cook.

109
00:04:51.058 --> 00:04:58.250
And so he say, okay, since everybody’s across that bridge or whatever, we’re going to have to just let the store close.

110
00:04:58.250 --> 00:05:01.519
And everybody, we’re going to move out to Lake Charles.

111
00:05:01.519 --> 00:05:07.077
We closed the store in 1965 and we all moved back to Lake Charles, which is closer.

112
00:05:07.077 --> 00:05:11.000
And during that time we had five kids in the family.

113
00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:11.307
<v MARSHALL>Yup.

114
00:05:11.307 --> 00:05:12.534
I’m the caboose. [laughs]

115
00:05:12.534 --> 00:05:18.362
<v ANDRUS>Three brothers and the caboose is right [unclear] Out of my three brothers, one of my brothers, they’re deceased.

116
00:05:18.362 --> 00:05:19.589
He died in 1999.

117
00:05:19.589 --> 00:05:21.736
But the other two are still living.

118
00:05:21.736 --> 00:05:23.270
And our grandparents, they deceased.

119
00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:26.337
Both sides, on my mother’s side and my father’s side.

120
00:05:26.337 --> 00:05:28.178
But we still have relatives here.

121
00:05:28.178 --> 00:05:31.245
Between here and Sulphur, and Westlake, we still got relatives.

122
00:05:31.245 --> 00:05:36.460
<v MARSHALL>And the ones that moved from here, sold the properties and stuff, they in Carlyss and Vinton.

123
00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:37.687
<v ANDRUS>They done moved everything.

124
00:05:37.687 --> 00:05:39.834
<v MARSHALL>But we still all keep in touch.

125
00:05:39.834 --> 00:05:46.583
<v ANDRUS>But you know, I hated [to leave?] Mossville when we left Mossville because the fact is, most of the relatives were here.

126
00:05:46.583 --> 00:05:49.650
And like I said, it was such a family-oriented place.

127
00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:53.025
Family was there to help you regardless what the situation was.

128
00:05:53.025 --> 00:05:53.331
<v MARSHALL>Right.

129
00:05:53.331 --> 00:05:55.785
<v ANDRUS>They never turned their back on each other.

130
00:05:55.785 --> 00:05:59.160
If you needed them, you just called them, they were there.

131
00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:01.000
<v MARSHALL>You could be a stranger.

132
00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:02.362
<v ANDRUS>You could a stranger.

133
00:06:02.362 --> 00:06:02.702
Neighbors.

134
00:06:02.702 --> 00:06:03.723
It didn’t matter.

135
00:06:03.723 --> 00:06:05.766
<v MARSHALL>That’s how it was out here.

136
00:06:05.766 --> 00:06:08.489
<v ANDRUS>People just came out to help each other.

137
00:06:08.489 --> 00:06:10.532
But now, you don’t have that.

138
00:06:10.532 --> 00:06:10.872
<v MARSHALL>No.

139
00:06:10.872 --> 00:06:17.000
<v ANDRUS>And that’s the best part that I’m going to miss, because the families are not oriented now.

140
00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:18.792
<v MARSHALL>It’s like family just separated from each other.

141
00:06:18.792 --> 00:06:20.584
It’s not like when we was coming up.

142
00:06:20.584 --> 00:06:21.480
Go outside and play.

143
00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:23.496
Go in the woods and hide and do things.

144
00:06:23.496 --> 00:06:25.288
Chase the chickens and stuff, you know. [laughs]

145
00:06:25.288 --> 00:06:29.992
<v ANDRUS>Oh, yeah, we got some good, oh, yeah, I got a chicken in my neck to remind me about it. [laughs]

146
00:06:29.992 --> 00:06:30.888
<v MARSHALL>She’s scared of feathers.

147
00:06:30.888 --> 00:06:31.112
<v ANDRUS>Was.

148
00:06:31.112 --> 00:06:33.352
<v MARSHALL>We used to run her all the time with feathers.

149
00:06:33.352 --> 00:06:34.920
<v ANDRUS>When my mom was pregnant with me—

150
00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:38.056
<v MARSHALL>When we wanted to get the best of her, to make her do something,

151
00:06:38.056 --> 00:06:41.192
we go outside and get a feather and we run all around the house.

152
00:06:41.192 --> 00:06:42.760
She go in the wood and stuff.

153
00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:45.000
But we wouldn’t go too far in the wood.

154
00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:50.314
<v ANDRUS>But when my mom was pregnant with me, my mom’s idea was to try to kill a rooster.

155
00:06:50.314 --> 00:06:52.086
And the rooster jumped at her.

156
00:06:52.086 --> 00:06:54.448
And when that rooster jumped at her, whatever—

157
00:06:54.448 --> 00:06:55.333
<v MARSHALL>She got marked.

158
00:06:55.333 --> 00:06:58.876
<v ANDRUS>I got marked with a bird mark in my neck. [laughter] So.

159
00:06:58.876 --> 00:07:01.533
So I got the bird mark of the rooster.

160
00:07:01.533 --> 00:07:04.781
But yeah then after that, they did, they did tease me.

161
00:07:04.781 --> 00:07:05.076
<v MARSHALL>[Agrees].

162
00:07:05.076 --> 00:07:07.733
We knew how to get the best of her.

163
00:07:07.733 --> 00:07:10.095
Especially when we didn’t want to do it.

164
00:07:10.095 --> 00:07:13.638
So she had to do it or else. [laughs] Get the feather.

165
00:07:13.638 --> 00:07:16.000
<v ANDRUS>I’m not like that anymore, thank God.

166
00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:17.398
<v MARSHALL>No. I hope not.

167
00:07:17.398 --> 00:07:19.494
<v ANDRUS>I’m not scared of a feather.

168
00:07:19.494 --> 00:07:22.988
<v MARSHALL>Not at your age, no more. [laughter] Can you imagine?

169
00:07:22.988 --> 00:07:27.530
<v ANDRUS>These old bones, these old bones can’t do what they used to do.

170
00:07:27.530 --> 00:07:29.277
But we had good times.

171
00:07:29.277 --> 00:07:30.325
We really did.

172
00:07:30.325 --> 00:07:31.723
We had good times.

173
00:07:31.723 --> 00:07:32.072
<v MARSHALL>Yeah.

174
00:07:32.072 --> 00:07:35.916
I wish things would come back like it used to be.

175
00:07:35.916 --> 00:07:37.663
<v ANDRUS>Especially with family being oriented.

176
00:07:37.663 --> 00:07:42.205
I mean, that’s what our country, communities need today, is families being oriented.

177
00:07:42.205 --> 00:07:42.554
<v MARSHALL>Yeah.

178
00:07:42.554 --> 00:07:45.000
I bought my property over here.

179
00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:46.576
<v ARSENEAULT>Can you describe your house?

180
00:07:46.576 --> 00:07:50.043
<v ANDRUS>We lived in a three-bedroom, one-bath, kitchen and a living room.

181
00:07:50.043 --> 00:07:54.457
And then like I said, the store was in the front of the house.

182
00:07:54.457 --> 00:07:56.978
We was in the back of the house.

183
00:07:56.978 --> 00:07:57.293
Mom—

184
00:07:57.293 --> 00:08:02.022
<v MARSHALL>When we first started out growing up, though, we didn't have bathrooms in the house.

185
00:08:02.022 --> 00:08:03.598
<v ANDRUS>No. We had an outhouse.

186
00:08:03.598 --> 00:08:06.120
<v MARSHALL>We had to go outside, outhouses and stuff.

187
00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:07.065
<v ANDRUS>Until they put—

188
00:08:07.065 --> 00:08:10.533
<v MARSHALL>Until they started with, you know, having bathrooms inside and stuff.

189
00:08:10.533 --> 00:08:14.000
<v ANDRUS>Can you imagine going to the outhouse when it’s dark?

190
00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:22.333
<v MARSHALL>Everybody used to say, we used to use the Sears Roebuck. [laughs] It’s just something people would say out here,

191
00:08:22.333 --> 00:08:31.917
when you go to the outdoor commode and stuff, they used to take the Sears Roebuck thing to wipe their behinds with. [laughs]

192
00:08:31.917 --> 00:08:33.167
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, the magazine.

193
00:08:33.167 --> 00:08:33.583
<v ANDRUS>Magazines.

194
00:08:33.583 --> 00:08:36.083
<v MARSHALL>Yes! [laughter] Oh, it was fun.

195
00:08:36.083 --> 00:08:39.000
We used to have fun, though.

196
00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:44.321
<v ANDRUS>Yeah, and in that time, we didn’t have, like she said, before they started putting the bathrooms in the house,

197
00:08:44.321 --> 00:08:49.110
you had the big number two, or the big number three tubs, you had to bathe in that.

198
00:08:49.110 --> 00:08:50.440
<v MARSHALL>Tubs to take baths, yeah.

199
00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:52.569
<v ANDRUS>You know, heat that water on the stove.

200
00:08:52.569 --> 00:08:53.633
<v MARSHALL>Everybody take their turn.

201
00:08:53.633 --> 00:08:55.229
<v ANDRUS>Everybody had to take their turns.

202
00:08:55.229 --> 00:08:55.761
<v MARSHALL>Well water.

203
00:08:55.761 --> 00:08:56.826
Everybody took their turn.

204
00:08:56.826 --> 00:09:01.881
When your well went out, everybody was out there, the neighborhood was out there helping you dig another well.

205
00:09:01.881 --> 00:09:02.413
<v ANDRUS>Oh, yeah.

206
00:09:02.413 --> 00:09:04.275
<v MARSHALL>That was the best thing behind it.

207
00:09:04.275 --> 00:09:05.073
Everybody helped everybody.

208
00:09:05.073 --> 00:09:06.670
And today you can’t get that.

209
00:09:06.670 --> 00:09:08.000
You can’t get it.

210
00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:08.363
<v ANDRUS>No.

211
00:09:08.363 --> 00:09:10.542
<v MARSHALL>I had bought property over here.

212
00:09:10.542 --> 00:09:12.721
Right around the corner from here.

213
00:09:12.721 --> 00:09:15.990
Because I wanted to raise my grandkids out here.

214
00:09:15.990 --> 00:09:19.985
But when Sasol came in, I went on and sold it.

215
00:09:19.985 --> 00:09:23.980
But I’m still going back out of the city living somewhere.

216
00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:27.975
Inside the city limits is no place to raise a kid.

217
00:09:27.975 --> 00:09:29.065
Especially small kids.

218
00:09:29.065 --> 00:09:30.881
There’s too many things happening.

219
00:09:30.881 --> 00:09:34.876
Get out of the city limit, way in the country somewhere.

220
00:09:34.876 --> 00:09:37.781
And you just can let them run free.

221
00:09:37.781 --> 00:09:39.597
They’ll stay out of trouble.

222
00:09:39.597 --> 00:09:39.960
City?

223
00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:41.050
They in trouble.

224
00:09:41.050 --> 00:09:42.502
I raised four kids.

225
00:09:42.502 --> 00:09:46.861
My oldest son going to be 40 on the thirteenth of June.

226
00:09:46.861 --> 00:09:51.219
My oldest daughter going to be 39 on the 27th of June.

227
00:09:51.219 --> 00:09:53.035
My youngest son is 37.

228
00:09:53.035 --> 00:09:55.940
He’ll be 37 on the second next month.

229
00:09:55.940 --> 00:10:01.025
And my baby is 35, which is just recovering from colon and liver cancer.

230
00:10:01.025 --> 00:10:05.383
I have 23 grandkids, two great-grandkids, and another great-grandkid on the way.

231
00:10:05.383 --> 00:10:09.015
My husband been a stroke victim for six years now.

232
00:10:09.015 --> 00:10:13.010
Recently he just fell and broke four bones in his back.

233
00:10:13.010 --> 00:10:16.279
So I take care of him and my daughter.

234
00:10:16.279 --> 00:10:17.368
And my grandbabies.

235
00:10:17.368 --> 00:10:19.547
And I love them to death.

236
00:10:19.547 --> 00:10:21.000
Keeps me busy.

237
00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:22.636
And we cook every day.

238
00:10:22.636 --> 00:10:24.927
If Nani don’t cook, they get mad.

239
00:10:24.927 --> 00:10:27.218
So Nani got to cook every day.

240
00:10:27.218 --> 00:10:34.418
And when I get ready to go to bed, they don’t want to go to bed with their mama, my little great-grandson.

241
00:10:34.418 --> 00:10:34.745
“Nani!

242
00:10:34.745 --> 00:10:35.073
Ni!

243
00:10:35.073 --> 00:10:35.400
Ni!”

244
00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:35.727
“What?”

245
00:10:35.727 --> 00:10:36.055
“Bottle.”

246
00:10:36.055 --> 00:10:37.691
I go fix his bottle.

247
00:10:37.691 --> 00:10:39.000
He’s two years old.

248
00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:42.273
He’ll climb into bed in between me and my husband.

249
00:10:42.273 --> 00:10:42.927
“Night night.”

250
00:10:42.927 --> 00:10:45.545
And he’ll lay there and go to sleep.

251
00:10:45.545 --> 00:10:50.455
I just pick him up and go put him back into bed with his mama.

252
00:10:50.455 --> 00:10:53.400
The little granddaughter is with my daughter, her grandma.

253
00:10:53.400 --> 00:11:00.600
She used to be real close to me, but she moved closer to my daughter now since she have cancer and stuff.

254
00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:01.582
But she’s three.

255
00:11:01.582 --> 00:11:04.200
She’ll be four on the third of April.

256
00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:06.491
She’s four but she’s like she’s 44.

257
00:11:06.491 --> 00:11:07.473
She’s so grown.

258
00:11:07.473 --> 00:11:12.382
And she told me the other day, “Nani, you see my mama fixed my hair.

259
00:11:12.382 --> 00:11:14.345
Don’t you think it look pretty?”

260
00:11:14.345 --> 00:11:16.309
And she knows, she loves to

261
00:11:16.309 --> 00:11:17.618
<c.p>take pictures of stuff.

262
00:11:17.618 --> 00:11:19.255
Oh, Jesus, lord have mercy.

263
00:11:19.255 --> 00:11:23.182
But like I told [unclear], we want to go ride some horses.

264
00:11:23.182 --> 00:11:24.818
So that’s what I said.

265
00:11:24.818 --> 00:11:33.000
I want to get out of the city limits, go out there, get some horses and stuff and show them what it’s all about.

266
00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:34.495
<v ARSENEAULT>What was y’all’s property like?

267
00:11:34.495 --> 00:11:35.692
<v MARSHALL>Oh, lordy have mercy.

268
00:11:35.692 --> 00:11:37.785
We have a big property out here.

269
00:11:37.785 --> 00:11:40.477
Whole big front yard, the side yard, the backyard.

270
00:11:40.477 --> 00:11:43.766
And you go a little further, it was nothing but woods.

271
00:11:43.766 --> 00:11:48.551
And boy, when we’d get in trouble, we’d go run and hide in the woods. [laughter]

272
00:11:48.551 --> 00:11:49.748
<v ANDRUS>Hide in the woods.

273
00:11:49.748 --> 00:11:50.944
Hide in the woods.

274
00:11:50.944 --> 00:11:52.738
<v MARSHALL>Mama and Daddy couldn’t find us.

275
00:11:52.738 --> 00:11:56.925
But we knew we had to come out by the time it get dark.

276
00:11:56.925 --> 00:11:59.318
Because you didn’t want to be outside, no,

277
00:11:59.318 --> 00:12:06.794
not after dark. [laughs] Anything could have came out of the wood and grabbed you. (laughs) I remember one we time was in the woods,

278
00:12:06.794 --> 00:12:08.290
we was climbing, acting crazy.

279
00:12:08.290 --> 00:12:13.374
My mama and my daddy used to tell us, “Don’t you go way far in the woods.”

280
00:12:13.374 --> 00:12:17.860
Because it was an old saying, they had some Indians that had died back there.

281
00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:19.654
And they had this wild horse.

282
00:12:19.654 --> 00:12:22.346
And it was like it would protect that area.

283
00:12:22.346 --> 00:12:22.645
<v ANDRUS>Area.

284
00:12:22.645 --> 00:12:24.140
<v MARSHALL>And we went that way.

285
00:12:24.140 --> 00:12:29.224
And honestly, one night, it just like just passed by us and you could see the footsteps,

286
00:12:29.224 --> 00:12:32.215
but you just felt the wind just pass by you.

287
00:12:32.215 --> 00:12:35.505
Well, talk about some kids ran out of that wood. [laughter]

288
00:12:35.505 --> 00:12:36.103
<v ANDRUS>Yeah, lord.

289
00:12:36.103 --> 00:12:37.000
<v MARSHALL>We ran.

290
00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:37.830
<v ARSENEAULT>What’s that story?

291
00:12:37.830 --> 00:12:42.809
<v ANDRUS>Well it’s, like she said, they say it’s like some Indians or somebody had died and they had,

292
00:12:42.809 --> 00:12:45.574
I guess they were protecting the land and everything else.

293
00:12:45.574 --> 00:12:47.234
We don’t know why or what.

294
00:12:47.234 --> 00:12:50.277
But like she said, we used to run in the woods.

295
00:12:50.277 --> 00:12:53.872
<v MARSHALL>They think they have some like some treasure or something buried back there.

296
00:12:53.872 --> 00:12:54.702
<v ANDRUS>On the land.

297
00:12:54.702 --> 00:12:56.085
But we never, nobody ever—

298
00:12:56.085 --> 00:13:03.000
<v MARSHALL>The horse and stuff, the spirit and stuff was just there to protect it, keep the people from taking the stuff from back there.

299
00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:03.429
<v ANDRUS>Oh, trust me.

300
00:13:03.429 --> 00:13:05.000
<v MARSHALL>We never did try to go back there to find nothing.

301
00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:05.571
<v ANDRUS>Oh, no, no, no.

302
00:13:05.571 --> 00:13:06.000
<v MARSHALL>Oh, no.

303
00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:07.822
<v ANDRUS>Once that horse came out and passed. . .

304
00:13:07.822 --> 00:13:09.123
<v MARSHALL>And you could hear it.

305
00:13:09.123 --> 00:13:09.904
<c.p>ANDRUS . . . we left.

306
00:13:09.904 --> 00:13:10.945
<v MARSHALL>You could hear it.

307
00:13:10.945 --> 00:13:13.548
You could see the footprints, but you couldn’t see him.

308
00:13:13.548 --> 00:13:14.589
<v ANDRUS>You couldn't see it.

309
00:13:14.589 --> 00:13:15.110
<v MARSHALL>Oh, yeah.

310
00:13:15.110 --> 00:13:16.671
That was the end of it.

311
00:13:16.671 --> 00:13:19.274
We never went back way down deep in the woods.

312
00:13:19.274 --> 00:13:20.836
<v ANDRUS>Not way deep in the woods.

313
00:13:20.836 --> 00:13:22.137
<v MARSHALL>Oh, no. Never no more.

314
00:13:22.137 --> 00:13:24.479
I wish I could go back to them days.

315
00:13:24.479 --> 00:13:25.000
<v ANDRUS>Yeah.

316
00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:26.755
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you all have a garden?

317
00:13:26.755 --> 00:13:27.633
<v MARSHALL>Oh, lord, yes.

318
00:13:27.633 --> 00:13:28.218
<v ANDRUS>Oh, yes.

319
00:13:28.218 --> 00:13:29.680
<v MARSHALL>We grew our own vegetables.

320
00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:30.265
<v ANDRUS>Own vegetables.

321
00:13:30.265 --> 00:13:32.313
<v MARSHALL>And most of the meat and stuff.

322
00:13:32.313 --> 00:13:32.898
Chicken, cows.

323
00:13:32.898 --> 00:13:33.190
Pigs.

324
00:13:33.190 --> 00:13:34.946
<v ANDRUS>We had our own little farm.

325
00:13:34.946 --> 00:13:35.238
<v MARSHALL>Yep.

326
00:13:35.238 --> 00:13:36.116
We loved it.

327
00:13:36.116 --> 00:13:39.333
<v ANDRUS>Which was more healthy for you than what it is today.

328
00:13:39.333 --> 00:13:39.626
<v MARSHALL>Yup.

329
00:13:39.626 --> 00:13:42.259
<v ANDRUS>Because they always fed like on corn and stuff.

330
00:13:42.259 --> 00:13:43.721
The meat was much better.

331
00:13:43.721 --> 00:13:48.986
You could tell it was much fresher because when you brown it and stuff it turned totally brown.

332
00:13:48.986 --> 00:13:49.279
<v MARSHALL>Gravy.

333
00:13:49.279 --> 00:13:52.789
<v ANDRUS>Today you got the white of it and that’s it. [laughter] Okay?

334
00:13:52.789 --> 00:13:54.252
There’s no taste to it.

335
00:13:54.252 --> 00:13:58.639
You’ve got to put a lot of stuff in it to make it taste good.

336
00:13:58.639 --> 00:14:05.952
But yeah, we did. [I go back?] And my mother and daddy always did the tomatoes, the cucumbers, the mustard greens, the okra, the potatoes.

337
00:14:05.952 --> 00:14:08.000
<v MARSHALL>You name it, she had it.

338
00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:09.243
<v ANDRUS>You name it, we had it.

339
00:14:09.243 --> 00:14:15.045
The only thing that I didn’t like about it is when you had to get out there in that doggone field and dig up the potatoes or whatever,

340
00:14:15.045 --> 00:14:16.288
or shuck the corn, or whatever.

341
00:14:16.288 --> 00:14:17.324
<v MARSHALL>But you had it good.

342
00:14:17.324 --> 00:14:18.568
<v ANDRUS>You sound like you’ve been there.

343
00:14:18.568 --> 00:14:19.604
<v MARSHALL>But you had it good!

344
00:14:19.604 --> 00:14:22.297
Since she was scared of feathers, she didn’t have to pick the chickens.

345
00:14:22.297 --> 00:14:23.333
<v ANDRUS>I didn’t pick the chickens.

346
00:14:23.333 --> 00:14:23.955
They picked it.

347
00:14:23.955 --> 00:14:24.162
Hello.

348
00:14:24.162 --> 00:14:24.991
She picked the chickens.

349
00:14:24.991 --> 00:14:26.027
<v MARSHALL>She wouldn’t pick the chickens!

350
00:14:26.027 --> 00:14:26.649
<v ANDRUS>I stayed away.

351
00:14:26.649 --> 00:14:27.892
<v MARSHALL>She was scared of the feathers.

352
00:14:27.892 --> 00:14:29.964
And I don't think that was fair at the time.

353
00:14:29.964 --> 00:14:31.000
No. It wasn’t fair.

354
00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:34.074
<v ANDRUS>I had a chicken mark in my neck to remind me!

355
00:14:34.074 --> 00:14:35.750
I didn’t want to do that.

356
00:14:35.750 --> 00:14:37.426
It was fair enough to us.

357
00:14:37.426 --> 00:14:39.941
<v MARSHALL>That’s why we chased you with the feathers. [laughter]

358
00:14:39.941 --> 00:14:41.338
<v ARSENEAULT>You all did have livestock?

359
00:14:41.338 --> 00:14:41.618
<v ANDRUS>Yes.

360
00:14:41.618 --> 00:14:42.176
We did.

361
00:14:42.176 --> 00:14:42.456
<v MARSHALL>Yeah.

362
00:14:42.456 --> 00:14:45.250
<v ANDRUS>Daddy always did, we did all our own butchering ourselves.

363
00:14:45.250 --> 00:14:48.882
Everybody had to be out there when they come down for the butchering.

364
00:14:48.882 --> 00:14:50.279
<v MARSHALL>Four o’clock in the morning.

365
00:14:50.279 --> 00:14:51.676
<v ANDRUS>Four o’clock in the morning.

366
00:14:51.676 --> 00:14:52.794
<v MARSHALL>Everybody, nobody in bed.

367
00:14:52.794 --> 00:14:53.353
Everybody up.

368
00:14:53.353 --> 00:14:53.912
Children, everybody.

369
00:14:53.912 --> 00:14:54.191
<v ANDRUS>Out.

370
00:14:54.191 --> 00:14:56.985
You got to get out there and do your share.

371
00:14:56.985 --> 00:14:58.662
<v MARSHALL>Everybody was outside doing a job.

372
00:14:58.662 --> 00:15:00.618
<v ANDRUS>Mama would make the boudin and stuff.

373
00:15:00.618 --> 00:15:02.853
Daddy would set out that black pot outside.

374
00:15:02.853 --> 00:15:05.647
<v MARSHALL>Ooh, I remember cleaning the guts out of the pigs.

375
00:15:05.647 --> 00:15:05.926
Ooooh!

376
00:15:05.926 --> 00:15:06.485
Yeah, yeah.

377
00:15:06.485 --> 00:15:09.000
<v ANDRUS>Make some good old hog cracklings and stuff.

378
00:15:09.000 --> 00:15:10.061
<v MARSHALL>That was fun.

379
00:15:10.061 --> 00:15:13.598
<v ANDRUS>Cook those big old black pot of that back bones.

380
00:15:13.598 --> 00:15:14.305
<v MARSHALL>Back bones.

381
00:15:14.305 --> 00:15:15.366
Oh, lord, yes.

382
00:15:15.366 --> 00:15:18.902
<v ANDRUS>With some good old mustard greens and yams and cornbread.

383
00:15:18.902 --> 00:15:27.390
Oh, there you go! [laughter] You know what I’m talking about, huh, brother? [laughter] But whatever we did, everybody would come in our neighborhood.

384
00:15:27.390 --> 00:15:31.988
Because either side, even across the street or whatever, it was all relatives.

385
00:15:31.988 --> 00:15:33.756
<v MARSHALL>It was all kin people.

386
00:15:33.756 --> 00:15:38.000
<v ANDRUS>It was always, everybody was kin on either side of us.

387
00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:47.116
<v MARSHALL>Holidays, special days, birthdays, weddings, anniversaries, everybody would [break] and you would never hear no noise.

388
00:15:47.116 --> 00:15:51.105
<v ANDRUS>Remember the time we had the wedding?

389
00:15:51.105 --> 00:15:54.523
And they was on horseback? [Verna?]

390
00:15:54.523 --> 00:15:55.663
<v MARSHALL>Oh, yes.

391
00:15:55.663 --> 00:16:00.221
I had a cousin got married western style.

392
00:16:00.221 --> 00:16:03.070
Oh, that was so beautiful.

393
00:16:03.070 --> 00:16:11.047
<v ANDRUS>They wanted to get married in a [break] And also we went to Westlake,

394
00:16:11.047 --> 00:16:18.453
to Saint John de Bosco and Our Lady of Prompt Succor in Sulphur.

395
00:16:18.453 --> 00:16:19.023
Yeah.

396
00:16:19.023 --> 00:16:21.302
We belong, we Catholic.

397
00:16:21.302 --> 00:16:24.721
We belong to the Catholic Church.

398
00:16:24.721 --> 00:16:27.000
<v MARSHALL>Love it. [laughs]

399
00:16:27.000 --> 00:16:29.571
<v ARSENEAULT>Who were some of the leaders in your church?

400
00:16:29.571 --> 00:16:34.714
<v ANDRUS>Most of the leaders in our church, my father is one, because he was Knights of Peter Claver.

401
00:16:34.714 --> 00:16:35.571
He’s one of—

402
00:16:35.571 --> 00:16:36.429
<v MARSHALL>A high knight.

403
00:16:36.429 --> 00:16:40.143
<v ANDRUS>Yeah, he was one of the high knights in Knights of Peter Claver.

404
00:16:40.143 --> 00:16:40.714
<v MARSHALL>Him and—

405
00:16:40.714 --> 00:16:41.571
<v ANDRUS>And we had—

406
00:16:41.571 --> 00:16:43.000
<v MARSHALL>--the majority of my uncles.

407
00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:43.571
My grandfather.

408
00:16:43.571 --> 00:16:47.000
<v ANDRUS>My uncles and them, they were all the Simiens or the Hardys.

409
00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:50.714
They were most of the high elites that we had in the church.

410
00:16:50.714 --> 00:16:53.286
<v MARSHALL>Everybody was, all the older people was in church.

411
00:16:53.286 --> 00:16:54.714
They was either a knight—

412
00:16:54.714 --> 00:16:56.429
<v ANDRUS>And same thing with the ladies.

413
00:16:56.429 --> 00:16:57.857
The ladies auxiliary and stuff.

414
00:16:57.857 --> 00:16:59.857
<v MARSHALL>The Ladies’ Auxiliary and stuff like that.

415
00:16:59.857 --> 00:17:01.000
<v ANDRUS>Whatever, they was all.

416
00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:03.000
<v MARSHALL>That’s how I was all raised.

417
00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:04.895
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did you go to school?

418
00:17:04.895 --> 00:17:08.368
<v ANDRUS>We all went to Sacred Heart Catholic School in Lake Charles.

419
00:17:08.368 --> 00:17:10.579
<v MARSHALL>Until they closed their high school down.

420
00:17:10.579 --> 00:17:11.526
<v ANDRUS>High school down.

421
00:17:11.526 --> 00:17:13.421
<v MARSHALL>Then we went to public school.

422
00:17:13.421 --> 00:17:14.053
<v ANDRUS>Public school.

423
00:17:14.053 --> 00:17:16.263
<v MARSHALL>We all went to the same school.

424
00:17:16.263 --> 00:17:21.316
Except my oldest brother had to go to Washington to finish his education in twelfth grade.

425
00:17:21.316 --> 00:17:25.421
And my brother that deceased in ’99, he graduated from Marion High School.

426
00:17:25.421 --> 00:17:27.632
<v ANDRUS>And which we all graduated from Marion.

427
00:17:27.632 --> 00:17:31.105
<v MARSHALL>And after that, all the rest of us graduated from Marion.

428
00:17:31.105 --> 00:17:33.000
But junior high, we went to—

429
00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:34.263
<v ANDRUS>It was called Lincoln.

430
00:17:34.263 --> 00:17:34.579
<v MARSHALL>Lincoln.

431
00:17:34.579 --> 00:17:36.158
But it’s [Lady Modo?] now.

432
00:17:36.158 --> 00:17:38.053
I never did go to college.

433
00:17:38.053 --> 00:17:39.947
I didn’t want to go college.

434
00:17:39.947 --> 00:17:41.211
I had enough school.

435
00:17:41.211 --> 00:17:44.053
I wanted to get married young, raise my family.

436
00:17:44.053 --> 00:17:45.000
And I did.

437
00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:46.263
Had my four kids.

438
00:17:46.263 --> 00:17:47.526
Two girls, two boys.

439
00:17:47.526 --> 00:17:49.421
And I love them to death.

440
00:17:49.421 --> 00:17:51.000
And they’re the best kids.

441
00:17:51.000 --> 00:17:53.211
I never had no trouble with them.

442
00:17:53.211 --> 00:17:55.421
Thank you, the good lord, for it.

443
00:17:55.421 --> 00:17:57.000
Lord have mercy. [laughs]

444
00:17:57.000 --> 00:17:58.799
<v ANDRUS>I did go to college.

445
00:17:58.799 --> 00:18:00.957
I went to McNeese State University.

446
00:18:00.957 --> 00:18:03.116
I stayed there for two years.

447
00:18:03.116 --> 00:18:04.915
I majored in business administration.

448
00:18:04.915 --> 00:18:13.189
And then some dude came and swept me off my feet and I didn’t go back. [laughs] Wind up with four lovely kids.

449
00:18:13.189 --> 00:18:14.628
Two girls, two boys.

450
00:18:14.628 --> 00:18:18.226
Out of those four, I had a set of twins.

451
00:18:18.226 --> 00:18:18.945
<v MARSHALL>Oh, yes.

452
00:18:18.945 --> 00:18:19.305
Indeed.

453
00:18:19.305 --> 00:18:21.463
<v ANDRUS>And my oldest daughter, she’s 39.

454
00:18:21.463 --> 00:18:23.262
My oldest son is 36.

455
00:18:23.262 --> 00:18:25.061
And my twins are 35.

456
00:18:25.061 --> 00:18:27.579
And I only got five little grandchildren.

457
00:18:27.579 --> 00:18:30.098
I’m so blessed to be with them.

458
00:18:30.098 --> 00:18:35.134
<v MARSHALL>Oh, you’re just jealous, because I got more than you. [laughter] But you know,

459
00:18:35.134 --> 00:18:40.890
at least mine, I got two, three just graduating that’s 18 this year, graduating in May.

460
00:18:40.890 --> 00:18:41.970
<v ANDRUS>Thank the lord!

461
00:18:41.970 --> 00:18:48.805
<v MARSHALL>I don't know how I’m going to separate my body like that, because they all go to different schools.

462
00:18:48.805 --> 00:18:55.280
<v ANDRUS>That was really that I love and everything else, it doesn’t matter how old we get or whatever.

463
00:18:55.280 --> 00:18:56.000
<v MARSHALL>No.

464
00:18:56.000 --> 00:19:00.658
<v ANDRUS>Families that was left and are still here, they still call you.

465
00:19:00.658 --> 00:19:04.928
They call you to participate in they children or children’s children.

466
00:19:04.928 --> 00:19:06.092
See they grandchildren.

467
00:19:06.092 --> 00:19:09.197
Because me and her, she’s known as Nana.

468
00:19:09.197 --> 00:19:11.526
I’m known as Nanny. (laughter) Okay.

469
00:19:11.526 --> 00:19:18.125
No matter if you’re cousin, niece, nephew, friends, or whatever, that’s what our whole community call us.

470
00:19:18.125 --> 00:19:19.289
That’s just it.

471
00:19:19.289 --> 00:19:24.724
Our parents raised us to be oriented to help people out and everything else.

472
00:19:24.724 --> 00:19:28.217
And I guess we just, we grew on people.

473
00:19:28.217 --> 00:19:31.711
Because people come to us for just about anything.

474
00:19:31.711 --> 00:19:35.204
I mean, we don’t turn our backs for nothing.

475
00:19:35.204 --> 00:19:37.145
I mean, I’ll feed you.

476
00:19:37.145 --> 00:19:38.309
It doesn’t matter.

477
00:19:38.309 --> 00:19:42.579
You don’t have it, I’ll give you a place to stay.

478
00:19:42.579 --> 00:19:43.355
That’s me.

479
00:19:43.355 --> 00:19:48.401
But you know, now that people, you can’t trust people too much anymore.

480
00:19:48.401 --> 00:19:55.000
But still, we do what we have to do because what our parents did into us.

481
00:19:55.000 --> 00:19:55.327
<v MARSHALL>Yeah.

482
00:19:55.327 --> 00:19:55.654
Yeah.

483
00:19:55.654 --> 00:19:59.578
Before I got disabled, I worked at Sears for about three years.

484
00:19:59.578 --> 00:20:05.464
Then after that, I went to the school board office, I started working for the school board office.

485
00:20:05.464 --> 00:20:08.081
And I started subbing for kids at school.

486
00:20:08.081 --> 00:20:10.370
And that’s where I learned about more

487
00:20:10.370 --> 00:20:15.602
<c.p>about, besides my kids, raising my kids, how these other kids, what was wrong with them.

488
00:20:15.602 --> 00:20:17.891
And I learned a lot about kids.

489
00:20:17.891 --> 00:20:23.123
They say kids today is, they like, I don't know, they disobedient because of the law.

490
00:20:23.123 --> 00:20:24.758
But it’s not just that.

491
00:20:24.758 --> 00:20:30.318
It’s because they don’t have the training at home and they don’t have the love at home.

492
00:20:30.318 --> 00:20:30.972
You know?

493
00:20:30.972 --> 00:20:35.550
It was one little boy, he was in the third grade with my daughter.

494
00:20:35.550 --> 00:20:38.493
That boy would give this teacher so much trouble.

495
00:20:38.493 --> 00:20:44.052
And that day I had to sub in that class and they had warned me about it.

496
00:20:44.052 --> 00:20:46.668
I said, I’m not worried about that child.

497
00:20:46.668 --> 00:20:48.630
I said, that child need attention.

498
00:20:48.630 --> 00:20:50.265
You all don’t understand that.

499
00:20:50.265 --> 00:20:53.863
So when I got in the classroom, he said, “Ms. Simien.”

500
00:20:53.863 --> 00:20:55.171
I said, “Yeah, what?

501
00:20:55.171 --> 00:20:56.152
What’s wrong, baby?”

502
00:20:56.152 --> 00:20:58.114
“Can I go to the bathroom?”

503
00:20:58.114 --> 00:21:00.403
I say, “You just got in class.

504
00:21:00.403 --> 00:21:04.000
I don't think you’ve got to go to the bathroom.”

505
00:21:04.000 --> 00:21:06.180
So he started cutting up, cutting up.

506
00:21:06.180 --> 00:21:08.361
Then the kids started picking at him.

507
00:21:08.361 --> 00:21:11.164
And I told him, I said, “Wait, come here.”

508
00:21:11.164 --> 00:21:14.902
And I took him outside the door and I closed the door.

509
00:21:14.902 --> 00:21:19.885
And I told the kids to read a book until I come back in the class.

510
00:21:19.885 --> 00:21:21.443
I say, “Baby, what’s wrong?”

511
00:21:21.443 --> 00:21:23.623
I say, “Why are you so frustrated?

512
00:21:23.623 --> 00:21:25.492
Why you got anger in you?”

513
00:21:25.492 --> 00:21:27.984
He say, “Ms. Simien, everybody picking at me.”

514
00:21:27.984 --> 00:21:28.918
I say, “Why?”

515
00:21:28.918 --> 00:21:32.656
“Because my mama was picked up for drugs and she in jail.”

516
00:21:32.656 --> 00:21:35.148
I say, “Baby, let me tell you something.

517
00:21:35.148 --> 00:21:36.705
Don’t let that bother you.

518
00:21:36.705 --> 00:21:40.443
Because they don’t know what their parents going to go through yet.

519
00:21:40.443 --> 00:21:42.000
Don’t let that bother you.

520
00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:43.557
Don’t pay attention to that.”

521
00:21:43.557 --> 00:21:51.656
I say, “Pray and ask God to lead you and to take care of your mama so she come out of jail to be with you.”

522
00:21:51.656 --> 00:21:58.508
And I think about three years after that, his mama came out of jail and he been back with her ever since.

523
00:21:58.508 --> 00:21:59.131
Just lovely.

524
00:21:59.131 --> 00:21:59.754
<v ANDRUS>It is.

525
00:21:59.754 --> 00:22:05.361
And that’s like with Mossville, the majority of our community in Mossville, everybody was family-oriented, like I said.

526
00:22:05.361 --> 00:22:06.918
But they were praying people.

527
00:22:06.918 --> 00:22:08.787
Everybody was praying people in Mossville.

528
00:22:08.787 --> 00:22:13.148
I mean, you could call anybody on the phone, go to their house or

529
00:22:13.148 --> 00:22:13.459
<c.p>whatever.

530
00:22:13.459 --> 00:22:20.000
If you needed prayers, if you needed somebody to talk to you or whatever for whatever reason, they were there.

531
00:22:20.000 --> 00:22:20.246
<v MARSHALL>Yeah.

532
00:22:20.246 --> 00:22:21.474
That was the best thing.

533
00:22:21.474 --> 00:22:24.421
<v ANDRUS>Never close their doors to say “I can’t help you” or nothing.

534
00:22:24.421 --> 00:22:24.667
<v MARSHALL>No.

535
00:22:24.667 --> 00:22:25.404
<v ANDRUS>They were there.

536
00:22:25.404 --> 00:22:28.105
Their doors were open for any reason that you would come.

537
00:22:28.105 --> 00:22:28.351
<v MARSHALL>Yeah.

538
00:22:28.351 --> 00:22:30.807
And that was the good thing about staying out here.

539
00:22:30.807 --> 00:22:32.772
You could leave your doors open at nighttime!

540
00:22:32.772 --> 00:22:33.509
And your windows.

541
00:22:33.509 --> 00:22:35.719
<v ANDRUS>Yeah, we used to leave doors open at night.

542
00:22:35.719 --> 00:22:36.947
Wake up in the morning.

543
00:22:36.947 --> 00:22:40.877
<v MARSHALL>But you can’t do it no more. [laughs] We had fun when we was coming up.

544
00:22:40.877 --> 00:22:41.368
<v ANDRUS>We did.

545
00:22:41.368 --> 00:22:42.105
<v MARSHALL>I loved it.

546
00:22:42.105 --> 00:22:44.561
<v ANDRUS>We might not look like we old, but we old.

547
00:22:44.561 --> 00:22:45.544
<v MARSHALL>You might be old.

548
00:22:45.544 --> 00:22:46.772
<v ANDRUS>I know, clothes get old.

549
00:22:46.772 --> 00:22:48.000
<v MARSHALL>Only clothes get old.

550
00:22:48.000 --> 00:22:53.565
<v ARSENEAULT>So when you were young in Mossville, where did you all gather?

551
00:22:53.565 --> 00:22:56.812
<v ANDRUS>At my mama house, at our house.

552
00:22:56.812 --> 00:22:57.739
<v MARSHALL>Everybody was—

553
00:22:57.739 --> 00:23:00.058
<v ANDRUS>Everything was at our house.

554
00:23:00.058 --> 00:23:07.478
At our house or we went to my Uncle Ernie’s house right there on Prater Road.

555
00:23:07.478 --> 00:23:07.942
<v MARSHALL>Yep.

556
00:23:07.942 --> 00:23:13.507
<v ANDRUS>Because we all had the biggest lands that everybody would get together.

557
00:23:13.507 --> 00:23:14.899
<v MARSHALL>We loved it.

558
00:23:14.899 --> 00:23:20.000
I’d give anything to go back to the days. [laughs]

559
00:23:20.000 --> 00:23:26.514
<v ARSENEAULT>So kind of wrapping up, we would love to sit down again with you and do a longer interview.

560
00:23:26.514 --> 00:23:30.629
But I wanted to ask you, what does home mean to you?

561
00:23:30.629 --> 00:23:31.657
<v ANDRUS>Being in Mossville?

562
00:23:31.657 --> 00:23:32.343
Home is—

563
00:23:32.343 --> 00:23:34.400
<v MARSHALL>It’s going to be really missed.

564
00:23:34.400 --> 00:23:35.429
<v ANDRUS>Yes, it is.

565
00:23:35.429 --> 00:23:37.829
It’s missed when we even left here.

566
00:23:37.829 --> 00:23:40.914
Because like we left part of our life here.

567
00:23:40.914 --> 00:23:45.371
And it’s a part that we couldn't take with us to Lake Charles.

568
00:23:45.371 --> 00:23:48.800
<v MARSHALL>And it’s something you have to get used to it.

569
00:23:48.800 --> 00:23:54.286
You know, when you’re, I just said in the country like, out here like it was,

570
00:23:54.286 --> 00:23:59.771
and you’re moving into a city that you never knew nothing about before, it’s completely different.

571
00:23:59.771 --> 00:24:00.114
<v ANDRUS>Yeah.

572
00:24:00.114 --> 00:24:07.657
<v MARSHALL>Because it’s a lot of things you can do out here in the country, you can’t do in the city and stuff.

573
00:24:07.657 --> 00:24:10.743
We have to get used to all this stuff.

574
00:24:10.743 --> 00:24:14.514
<v ANDRUS>And the community is not like the same like being here.

575
00:24:14.514 --> 00:24:17.257
<v MARSHALL>The community’s not like even, it’s completely different.

576
00:24:17.257 --> 00:24:20.343
And as years go, it’s getting worse and worse.

577
00:24:20.343 --> 00:24:23.771
You know, things are not like it used to be.

578
00:24:23.771 --> 00:24:31.314
Like I say, I guess pray and ask God for things to come back and be better than what it is now.

579
00:24:31.314 --> 00:24:32.000
Who knows?

580
00:24:32.000 --> 00:24:33.029
<v ANDRUS>Families helping families.

581
00:24:33.029 --> 00:24:34.057
That’s the best.

582
00:24:34.057 --> 00:24:36.114
<v MARSHALL>They don’t help families no more.

583
00:24:36.114 --> 00:24:37.829
<v ANDRUS>That was the best time.

584
00:24:37.829 --> 00:24:39.200
Sit around the fire.

585
00:24:39.200 --> 00:24:39.543
<v MARSHALL>Yep.

586
00:24:39.543 --> 00:24:41.257
<v ANDRUS>Listen to the old stories.

587
00:24:41.257 --> 00:24:44.000
Listen to the old people talking Creole.

588
00:24:44.000 --> 00:24:44.538
<v MARSHALL>Dancing Zydeco.

589
00:24:44.538 --> 00:24:45.077
<v ANDRUS>Dancing Zydeco.

590
00:24:45.077 --> 00:24:45.615
<v MARSHALL>Yes, indeed.

591
00:24:45.615 --> 00:24:47.769
<v ANDRUS>Oh, can we remember the [unclear] over here?

592
00:24:47.769 --> 00:24:48.846
When Cousin [Buzu Chavez?]

593
00:24:48.846 --> 00:24:49.385
<v MARSHALL>Yes, indeed.

594
00:24:49.385 --> 00:24:51.538
<v ANDRUS>We used to hang out at [Buzu?] house.

595
00:24:51.538 --> 00:24:52.615
<v MARSHALL>And we always danced.

596
00:24:52.615 --> 00:24:54.500
<v ANDRUS>And everybody had their fun and danced.

597
00:24:54.500 --> 00:24:59.615
<v MARSHALL>My mom and my daddy would go to the dance, he would take me and my sister with them.

598
00:24:59.615 --> 00:25:00.154
<v ANDRUS>Oh, yeah.

599
00:25:00.154 --> 00:25:02.577
We had to go to the dance with them.

600
00:25:02.577 --> 00:25:05.000
<v MARSHALL>The girls would never stay home by themselves.

601
00:25:05.000 --> 00:25:06.143
<v ANDRUS>Girls never stayed home.

602
00:25:06.143 --> 00:25:08.143
Girls had to follow Mom and Dad.

603
00:25:08.143 --> 00:25:09.857
Boys, they all had to work.

604
00:25:09.857 --> 00:25:11.286
They were older than us.

605
00:25:11.286 --> 00:25:13.286
They had to work to help out.

606
00:25:13.286 --> 00:25:17.000
But me and my sister, we had to go to dance with them.

607
00:25:17.000 --> 00:25:18.429
There were some good times.

608
00:25:18.429 --> 00:25:19.286
We enjoyed it.

609
00:25:19.286 --> 00:25:21.571
Even when Cousin [Buzu?] was down the road.

610
00:25:21.571 --> 00:25:23.571
Sitting on his porch or his accordion.

611
00:25:23.571 --> 00:25:25.571
And Daddy come there with his accordion.

612
00:25:25.571 --> 00:25:27.000
And then what’s it called?

613
00:25:27.000 --> 00:25:27.571
<v MARSHALL>His harmonica.

614
00:25:27.571 --> 00:25:28.143
<v ANDRUS>His harmonica.

615
00:25:28.143 --> 00:25:32.143
Or what you call him would come with this rub board, [come backing them?].

616
00:25:32.143 --> 00:25:33.571
That was some good days.

617
00:25:33.571 --> 00:25:34.143
<v MARSHALL>Mm hmm.

618
00:25:34.143 --> 00:25:35.286
<v ANDRUS>We had some fun.

619
00:25:35.286 --> 00:25:35.571
<v MARSHALL>Yup.

620
00:25:35.571 --> 00:25:37.857
That was the good days about out here.

621
00:25:37.857 --> 00:25:38.429
<v ANDRUS>Mm hmm.

622
00:25:38.429 --> 00:25:40.714
<v MARSHALL>You could party from sun up to sundown.

623
00:25:40.714 --> 00:25:41.571
<v ANDRUS>It didn’t matter.

624
00:25:41.571 --> 00:25:43.571
<v MARSHALL>You didn’t have to worry about nothing.

625
00:25:43.571 --> 00:25:45.857
<v ANDRUS>Family would come in, people would pass by.

626
00:25:45.857 --> 00:25:48.429
Cousin [Buzu?] and Daddy said, “Come on, come on.

627
00:25:48.429 --> 00:25:49.571
Invite them on in.”

628
00:25:49.571 --> 00:25:53.000
Everybody would just sit there and just have a good time.

629
00:25:53.000 --> 00:25:53.685
<v MARSHALL>Love it.

630
00:25:53.685 --> 00:25:56.767
I would give anything to go back to it.

631
00:25:56.767 --> 00:25:58.822
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you all remember any songs?

632
00:25:58.822 --> 00:26:01.219
<v MARSHALL>Oh my God, “Paper in My Shoe.”

633
00:26:01.219 --> 00:26:02.589
<v ANDRUS>“Paper in My Shoe.”

634
00:26:02.589 --> 00:26:03.274
“Hey Lucille.”

635
00:26:03.274 --> 00:26:06.356
That’s what Daddy and Cousin [Buzu?] loved to play.

636
00:26:06.356 --> 00:26:08.068
<v MARSHALL>“Knocking on the Back Door.”

637
00:26:08.068 --> 00:26:09.781
<v ANDRUS>“Knocking on the Back Door.”

638
00:26:09.781 --> 00:26:12.521
Like I say, we had some good days.

639
00:26:12.521 --> 00:26:15.260
<v MARSHALL>There you have some names of them songs.

640
00:26:15.260 --> 00:26:15.945
<v ANDRUS>Oh, yeah.

641
00:26:15.945 --> 00:26:16.973
That was great.

642
00:26:16.973 --> 00:26:18.000
<v MARSHALL>Love it.

643
00:26:18.000 --> 00:26:25.147
<v ARSENEAULT>What is the most important thing you want people to remember about Mossville that isn’t in a history book?

644
00:26:25.147 --> 00:26:25.523
<v MARSHALL>Hmm.

645
00:26:25.523 --> 00:26:28.532
<v ANDRUS>Me, personally, like I said, it’s just family-oriented.

646
00:26:28.532 --> 00:26:30.789
The community being family helping families.

647
00:26:30.789 --> 00:26:34.174
Because now like everybody’s gone here and there, scattered.

648
00:26:34.174 --> 00:26:37.183
Families is not there for each other anymore.

649
00:26:37.183 --> 00:26:40.569
And I’m going to miss that, being in Mossville.

650
00:26:40.569 --> 00:26:41.697
I really am.

651
00:26:41.697 --> 00:26:48.092
And I know a lot of them miss the little store my daddy and Ms. Martill had,

652
00:26:48.092 --> 00:26:53.358
because they was the only two black people who had little stores in Mossville.

653
00:26:53.358 --> 00:26:56.367
And then Ms. Martill closed hers before we

654
00:26:56.367 --> 00:26:58.624
<c.p>closed ours because she took sick.

655
00:26:58.624 --> 00:27:04.266
And I know a lot of people are going to miss that just as well.

656
00:27:04.266 --> 00:27:11.413
Because Daddy was a person that, he didn’t give a care if you ain’t had the money or nothing.

657
00:27:11.413 --> 00:27:16.679
If you needed something for your children, come get it out of his store.

658
00:27:16.679 --> 00:27:17.807
Pay him later.

659
00:27:17.807 --> 00:27:20.064
<v MARSHALL>Or don’t pay him at all.

660
00:27:20.064 --> 00:27:21.945
<v ANDRUS>Or don’t him at all.

661
00:27:21.945 --> 00:27:24.578
Because he was planning on give you—

662
00:27:24.578 --> 00:27:28.339
<v MARSHALL>Never say “God,” say “give,” and you get your blessing.

663
00:27:28.339 --> 00:27:29.092
<v ANDRUS>That’s right.

664
00:27:29.092 --> 00:27:33.229
He wasn’t going to let nobody go starving, or anything else.

665
00:27:33.229 --> 00:27:33.606
So.

666
00:27:33.606 --> 00:27:37.743
But you don’t do that now with the society right now.

667
00:27:37.743 --> 00:27:39.248
You can’t do that.

668
00:27:39.248 --> 00:27:40.000
So.

669
00:27:40.000 --> 00:27:42.933
<v ARSENEAULT>Well thank you all so much.

670
00:27:42.933 --> 00:27:43.911
<v MARSHALL>You’re welcome.

671
00:27:43.911 --> 00:27:44.889
<v ANDRUS>Thank you.

672
00:27:44.889 --> 00:27:46.844
<v ARSENEAULT>This concludes our interview.

673
00:27:46.844 --> 00:27:54.178
On behalf of the center and of the Calcasieu Museum, we appreciate your contribution today.

674
00:27:54.178 --> 00:27:57.111
Did you want to add anything?

675
00:27:57.111 --> 00:27:57.600
Closing?

676
00:27:57.600 --> 00:27:58.089
<v MARSHALL>You?

677
00:27:58.089 --> 00:27:58.578
<v ANDRUS>No.

678
00:27:58.578 --> 00:27:59.067
<v ARSENEAULT>Great.

679
00:27:59.067 --> 00:28:02.000
Well, this concludes our interview.

680
00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:02.200
<c.p>[End Tape 4426.

681
00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:02.200
End Session I.]
